 "author_user_id": 492716,
 "author_display_name": "onemasse",
 "author_id_missing": false,
 "permalink": "https://stackoverflow.com/questions/4255598/delete-vs-delete",
 "body_html": "<blockquote>\n<p><strong>Possible Duplicate:</strong><br />\n<a href=\"https://stackoverflow.com/q/2425728/5740428\">What is the difference between delete and delete[]?</a></p>\n</blockquote>\n\n<p>When I was taught C++, this was a long time ago. I was told to never use <code>delete</code> but <code>delete[]</code> as performing <code>delete[]</code> on a single object will be equivalent to <code>delete</code>. Knowing not to trust teachers too much I wonder, Is this true?</p>\n<p>Is there ever a reason to call <code>delete</code> instead of <code>delete[]</code>?</p>\n<p>I've scanned the possibly related questions in SO, but haven't found any clear answer.</p>\n",
 "passes": [
  "main"
 ],
 "data_source": "so_main_cached (2026-10-05 collection)",
 "in_main_retained_480": true,
 "in_main_nonNA_coded_346": true,
 "in_offcwe_nonNA_coded": false,
 "events": [
  {
   "post_type": "answer",
   "post_id": 4255622,
   "parent_id": 4255598,
   "author_user_id": 366832,
   "author_display_name": "jmpcm",
   "author_id_missing": false,
   "role": "responder",
   "is_accepted": false,
   "permalink": "https://stackoverflow.com/a/4255622",
   "score": 11,
   "created_utc": "2010-11-23T11:37:05Z",
   "last_edit_utc": "2016-04-10T00:03:53Z",
   "last_activity_utc": "2016-04-10T00:03:53Z",
   "license": "CC BY-SA 3.0",
   "body_html": "<p><code>delete</code> is used to delete a single object, while <code>delete[]</code> is used to delete an array of objects. Check this <a href=\"http://www.cplusplus.com/reference/std/new/operator%20delete%5B%5D/\" rel=\"nofollow noreferrer\">link</a> for more info.</p>\n"
  },
  {
   "post_type": "comment",
   "post_id": 4610451,
   "parent_id": 4255598,
   "parent_type": "question",
   "author_user_id": 121332,
   "author_display_name": "Alex Brown",
   "author_id_missing": false,
   "role": "responder",
   "is_accepted": null,
   "permalink": "https://stackoverflow.com/questions/4255598/#comment4610451_4255598",
   "score": 24,
   "created_utc": "2010-11-23T11:37:31Z",
   "edited": false,
   "license": "CC BY-SA 2.5",
   "body_html": "/me throws up a little.  Did they recommend you always call new[1] as well?"
  },
  {
   "post_type": "answer",
   "post_id": 4255629,
   "parent_id": 4255598,
   "author_user_id": 347137,
   "author_display_name": "Simone",
   "author_id_missing": false,
   "role": "responder",
   "is_accepted": false,
   "permalink": "https://stackoverflow.com/a/4255629",
   "score": 36,
   "created_utc": "2010-11-23T11:38:23Z",
   "last_edit_utc": null,
   "last_activity_utc": "2010-11-23T11:38:23Z",
   "license": "CC BY-SA 2.5",
   "body_html": "<p>No! you call <code>delete[]</code> when you allocate with <code>new[]</code>, otherwise you call <code>delete</code>.</p>\n\n<p>What teacher told you leads to undefined behaviour and, if you are lucky, an application crash.</p>\n"
  },
  {
   "post_type": "answer",
   "post_id": 4255636,
   "parent_id": 4255598,
   "author_user_id": 451980,
   "author_display_name": "icecrime",
   "author_id_missing": false,
   "role": "responder",
   "is_accepted": true,
   "permalink": "https://stackoverflow.com/a/4255636",
   "score": 119,
   "created_utc": "2010-11-23T11:38:43Z",
   "last_edit_utc": null,
   "last_activity_utc": "2010-11-23T11:38:43Z",
   "license": "CC BY-SA 2.5",
   "body_html": "<p>From the standard (5.3.5/2) :</p>\n\n<blockquote>\n  <p>In the first alternative (delete\n  object), the value of the operand of\n  delete shall be a pointer to a\n  non-array object or a pointer to a\n  sub-object (1.8) representing a base\n  class of such an object (clause 10).\n  <strong>If not, the behavior is undefined.</strong></p>\n  \n  <p>In the second alternative (delete\n  array), the value of the operand of\n  delete shall be the pointer value\n  which resulted from a previous array\n  new-expression. <strong>If not, the\n  behavior is undefined.</strong></p>\n</blockquote>\n\n<p>So no : they are in no way equivalent !</p>\n"
  },
  {
   "post_type": "answer",
   "post_id": 4255641,
   "parent_id": 4255598,
   "author_user_id": 112950,
   "author_display_name": "Alex Budovski",
   "author_id_missing": false,
   "role": "responder",
   "is_accepted": false,
   "permalink": "https://stackoverflow.com/a/4255641",
   "score": 55,
   "created_utc": "2010-11-23T11:39:21Z",
   "last_edit_utc": null,
   "last_activity_utc": "2010-11-23T11:39:21Z",
   "license": "CC BY-SA 2.5",
   "body_html": "<p><code>delete []</code> is \"vector delete\" and corresponds to vector new, i.e. <code>new[]</code>.</p>\n\n<p>You must use the matching pair of allocators. E.g. <code>malloc/free</code>, <code>new/delete</code>, <code>new[]/delete[]</code>, else you get undefined behavior.</p>\n"
  },
